WEBVTT

00:01.000 --> 00:07.700
A neural test reaches its main result on day twelve.
MEACompass asks two questions.

00:07.700 --> 00:16.600
Can day-seven measurements predict that result for a new chemical?
And can the system refuse when it is not confident?

00:16.600 --> 00:22.300
This demo uses held-out examples.
OBSERVED shows early measurements.

00:22.300 --> 00:28.900
PREDICTED shows the day-twelve forecast,
uncertainty interval, and baseline.

00:28.900 --> 00:36.400
The reliability badge says accept or abstain.
High uncertainty means continue the experiment.

00:36.400 --> 00:40.400
HYPOTHESIS marks what we have not validated.

00:40.400 --> 00:48.600
We studied 136 chemicals and about 4,200
complete records across time.

00:48.600 --> 00:53.800
To prevent leakage, all doses and repeats
from one chemical stay together.

00:53.800 --> 01:04.200
We tested firing rate, bursts, active electrodes,
network spikes, and coordinated activity.

01:04.200 --> 01:17.900
MAE fell by 14.2–39.0% versus BT+,
and by 12.3–42.9% versus BT++.

01:17.900 --> 01:23.600
Confidence intervals across chemicals
excluded zero for all five endpoints.

01:23.600 --> 01:31.900
A registered negative control failed,
so our rule stopped the pipeline. We show this failure.

01:31.900 --> 01:37.500
A limited audit found no future information or alignment error.
A clean target shuffle worked correctly.

01:37.500 --> 01:48.600
The real model beat all twenty independent block-null runs.
We continued with safeguards and the stronger BT++ comparison.

01:48.600 --> 01:59.600
Nominal 90% intervals achieved
91.0–92.4% empirical coverage.

01:59.600 --> 02:06.500
Selective prediction passed three of five endpoints:
firing rate, active electrodes, and coordinated activity.

02:06.500 --> 02:17.300
Bursts and network spikes did not pass and remain limitations.
This is research decision support only.

02:17.300 --> 02:27.100
Day-five data alone were weaker.
Adding day-seven physiology gave the main improvement.

02:27.100 --> 02:35.600
For accepted cases, the forecast is available five days
before the day-twelve result.

02:35.600 --> 02:43.000
Day-seven physiology was the strongest feature group.
Chemical information also helped. These are predictions, not causes.

02:45.200 --> 02:53.100
A reliable system must show mistakes.
The demo includes one correct case and one declared failure.

02:53.100 --> 03:02.700
We chose both before recording, not because they looked better.
The uncertainty and reliability decisions stay visible.

03:02.700 --> 03:10.600
A secondary test retained predictive value
when we held out either NTP or ToxCast.

03:10.600 --> 03:17.200
The model was not retuned.
This is not transfer to another laboratory or device.

03:17.300 --> 03:22.700
The data come from rat cortical neural MEA tests.

03:22.700 --> 03:29.100
They are not human or organ-on-chip data.
A new platform needs local training,

03:29.100 --> 03:38.400
recalibration, and prospective validation.
MEACompass is open and reproducible.

03:38.400 --> 03:47.100
It turns early measurements into a forecast,
an uncertainty interval, and an honest option to wait.

03:47.100 --> 03:51.900
Forecast when evidence is strong.
Continue the experiment when it is not.
